id Complex `JOIN`s in RLS Policies\n* **Problem**: Joining roles or permissions tables inside an RLS policy runs the join for every candidate row.\n* **Fix**: Encode roles, organization IDs, or permissions into the user's JWT metadata (`app_metadata`) using Supabase Custom Claims or Auth Hooks.\n  ```sql\n  -- Reading directly from cached JWT instead of querying an extra table\n  CREATE POLICY \"Tenant isolation\" ON patient_records\n    FOR SELECT TO authenticated\n    USING (hospital_id = (SELECT (auth.jwt() -> 'app_metadata' ->> 'hospital_id')::uuid));\n  ```\n\n#### 4. Explicitly Specify Roles (`TO authenticated`)\n* Omitting the role target causes the policy to evaluate for every role, including `anon`. Always add `TO authenticated` to skip policy evaluation for unauthenticated users.\n\n#### 5. Service Role Key Bypasses RLS\n* The `service_role` key completely bypasses RLS policies. It must **never** be exposed in client code (only in secure server-side environments or Supabase Edge Functions).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tesseract.js (Latest, Hindi + English OCR, Memory Management)\n\n### Exact Packages & Latest Versions\n| Package | Role | Version |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `tesseract.js` | JS wrapper for Tesseract WASM | `^6.0.1` |\n| `tesseract.js-core` | WebAssembly engine compiled from C++ | `^6.1.2` |\n\n---\n\n### Hindi + English OCR Setup\n\nIn Tesseract.js v5 and v6, you initialize multiple languages by passing an array of ISO 639-2 language codes (`['hin', 'eng']`) to `createWorker`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { createWorker } from 'tesseract.js';\n\nexport async function extractHindiEnglishText(imageSource: File | Blob | string) {\n  // Pass an array of language codes\n  const worker = await createWorker(['hin', 'eng'], 1, {\n    logger: (progress) => {\n      console.log(`[OCR Status] ${progress.status}: ${Math.round((progress.progress || 0) * 100)}%`);\n    },\n  });\n\n  try {\n    const { data: { text, confidence } } = await worker.recognize(imageSource);\n    return { text, confidence };\n  } finally {\n    // Terminate worker if not using singleton\n    await worker.terminate();\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Memory Management Concerns & Gotchas\n\n* **WebAssembly Memory Leaks (Do Not Re-create Workers)**:\n  * Creating a new worker via `createWorker()` for each OCR task allocates a WebAssembly memory buffer that may not be garbage-collected immediately. Recreating workers repeatedly on a single page will exhaust RAM and crash mobile browser tabs.\n  * **Best Practice**: Implement a **Singleton Worker** pattern. Initialize one worker, reuse it across multiple recognition calls, and only call `await worker.terminate()` when the feature or page unmounts.\n* **Large Traineddata Download Sizes**:\n  * `hin.traineddata` (~11-23 MB) + `eng.traineddata` (~4-15 MB) total ~15-38 MB over the wire.\n  * By default, files are downloaded from jsDelivr/GitHub CDN and cached in browser IndexedDB.\n  * On mobile or slow 3G/4G connections, network timeouts can cause recognition to hang. Always display an explicit download progress indicator to the user.\n* **Camera Image Resolution**:\n  * High-resolution images directly from smartphone cameras (12–48 megapixels) quickly cause WebAssembly Out-Of-Memory (OOM) errors.\n  * **Fix**: Scale down images on an HTML `<canvas>` to a maximum resolution of 1800–2400px width/height and apply grayscale/contrast thresholding before passing to `worker.recognize()`.\n* **Offline / PWA Hosting**:\n  * If the app is run offline or behind strict firewalls, host `worker.min.js`, `tesseract-core.wasm.js`, and `*.traineddata.gz` in your `public/` directory and configure `workerPath`, `corePath`, and `langPath` in `createWorker`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. react-i18next (Hindi/English Toggle with Lazy Loading)\n\n### Exact Packages & Latest Versions\n| Package | Role | Version |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `i18next` | Core Internationalization framework | `^26.4.2` |\n| `react-i18next` | React 19 bindings & hooks | `^17.0.15` |\n| `i18next-http-backend` | Lazy loads translation JSON over HTTP | `^4.0.2` |\n| `i18next-browser-languagedetector` | Detects & caches user language | `^8.2.1` |\n\n---\n\n### Setup Pattern\n\n#### 1. Folder Structure for Translation Files\nPlace translation files in the Vite `public/` directory so they are fetched on demand:\n```\npublic/\n  locales/\n    en/\n      common.json\n    hi/\n      common.json\n```\n\n#### 2. i18n Configuration (`src/i18n.ts`)\n```typescript\nimport i18n from 'i18next';\nimport { initReactI18next } from 'react-i18next';\nimport HttpBackend from 'i18next-http-backend';\nimport LanguageDetector from 'i18next-browser-languagedetector';\n\ni18n\n  .use(HttpBackend)\n  .use(LanguageDetector)\n  .use(initReactI18next)\n  .init({\n    fallbackLng: 'en',\n    supportedLngs: ['en', 'hi'],\n    ns: ['common'],\n    defaultNS: 'common',\n    backend: {\n      // Lazy loads JSON files on demand from public directory\n      loadPath: '/locales/{{lng}}/{{ns}}.json',\n    },\n    detection: {\n      order: ['localStorage', 'navigator'],\n      caches: ['localStorage'],\n    },\n    react: {\n      useSuspense: true, // Native React 19 Suspense support\n    },\n    interpolation: {\n      escapeValue: false, // React automatically escapes XSS\n    },\n  });\n\nexport default i18n;\n```\n\n#### 3. React 19 Root with `<Suspense>` (`src/App.tsx`)\n```tsx\nimport { Suspense } from 'react';\nimport './i18n';\nimport { useTranslation } from 'react-i18next';\n\nexport function LanguageToggle() {\n  const { i18n, t } = useTranslation('common');\n\n  const toggleLanguage = () => {\n    const nextLang = i18n.language.startsWith('hi') ? 'en' : 'hi';\n    i18n.changeLanguage(nextLang);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"flex items-center gap-4\">\n      <button\n        onClick={toggleLanguage}\n        className=\"px-4 py-2 bg-blue-600 text-white rounded font-medium\"\n      >\n        {i18n.language.startsWith('hi') ? 'English' : 'हिंदी'}\n      </button>\n      <p>{t('welcomeMessage')}</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<div className=\"p-8 text-center\">Loading translations...</div>}>\n      <LanguageToggle />\n    </Suspense>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Gotchas\n* **Devanagari Font Support**: Devanagari script (used for Hindi) requires proper Unicode font ligatures. Ensure your CSS loads a font that supports Devanagari (e.g. `Noto Sans Devanagari` or system fonts `system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif`), otherwise glyph rendering can break.\n* **Suspense Flash on Language Switch**: Switching language triggers React Suspense while the new language JSON is fetched. Place `<Suspense>` locally around translated components rather than wrapping the entire application tree to avoid full-screen blanking.\n* **Vite Base Path**: If your Vite app is deployed to a sub-path (e.g., `base: '/my-app/'`), adjust `loadPath` to `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}locales/{{lng}}/{{ns}}.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Web Speech API (Hindi Voice Input & Limitations)\n\n### Browser Compatibility Matrix for `hi-IN`\n| Browser | Engine | `hi-IN` (Hindi) Support | Architecture |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Google Chrome (Desktop & Android)** | Chromium | **Excellent** | Cloud-based (streams audio to Google Speech servers) |\n| **Microsoft Edge** | Chromium | **Good** | Cloud-based (streams audio to Microsoft Speech servers) |\n| **Safari (iOS & macOS)** | WebKit | **Unreliable / Inconsistent** | Siri on-device engine (often disabled or failing in PWAs/WebViews) |\n| **Mozilla Firefox** | Gecko | **Unsupported** | Disabled by default in production |\n\n---\n\n### Key Implementation Pattern\n```typescript\nexport function setupHindiSpeechRecognition(\n  onResult: (transcript: string, isFinal: boolean) => void,\n  onError: (err: string) => void\n) {\n  const SpeechRecognition =\n    (window as any).SpeechRecognition || (window as any).webkitSpeechRecognition;\n\n  if (!SpeechRecognition) {\n    onError('Web Speech API is not supported in this browser.');\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  const recognition = new SpeechRecognition();\n  recognition.lang = 'hi-IN'; // Standard BCP-47 tag for Hindi (India)\n  recognition.continuous = false; // Prevents unpredictable mobile browser timeouts\n  recognition.interimResults = true; // Provides real-time typing feedback\n\n  recognition.onresult = (event: any) => {\n    let interimTranscript = '';\n    let finalTranscript = '';\n\n    for (let i = event.resultIndex; i < event.results.length; ++i) {\n      if (event.results[i].isFinal) {\n        finalTranscript += event.results[i][0].transcript;\n      } else {\n        interimTranscript += event.results[i][0].transcript;\n      }\n    }\n\n    onResult(finalTranscript || interimTranscript, Boolean(finalTranscript));\n  };\n\n  recognition.onerror = (event: any) => {\n    onError(event.error);\n  };\n\n  return recognition;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Critical Limitations & Gotchas\n* **Network Dependency (Not Offline)**: Chromium and Safari do not perform client-side speech-to-text locally. They capture audio and send PCM frames to Google/Apple servers. If the user is offline, the API fails immediately with a `network` error.\n* **Code-Switching (\"Hinglish\")**: In Indian healthcare and daily contexts, users frequently mix Hindi and English (\"Mera blood pressure check karna hai\"). The `hi-IN` model will transcribe English words phonetically into Devanagari (\"ब्लड प्रेशर\") or produce mistranscriptions. The Web Speech API cannot listen to dual language models simultaneously.\n* **Chrome Silence Timeout**: In Google Chrome, recognition will automatically abort and fire `onend` after 3–5 seconds of silence, even with `continuous = true`. Production apps need an explicit state machine to auto-restart the listener when desired.\n* **HTTPS & Explicit User Gesture**: Microphones cannot be activated programmatically on page load. Recognition can only start from a direct user action (click/touch event) within an HTTPS origin (or `localhost`).\n* **Production Recommendation**: If cross-browser compatibility (Firefox, iOS PWA, mobile WebViews) or high medical accuracy is required, use a backend speech-to-text proxy (e.g., **Bhashini API** [Govt of India AI], **Google Cloud Speech-to-Text**, or **Whisper**) rather than the client-side Web Speech API.\n\n---\n\n## 6. QR Code Generation in React\n\n### Recommended Library\n**`qrcode.react` (Latest Version: `4.2.0`)**\n* The industry standard for React.\n* Zero external native dependencies, supports both SVG (`QRCodeSVG`) and Canvas (`QRCodeCanvas`), and includes built-in logo excavation (`imageSettings`).\n\n---\n\n### Setup Pattern\n\n```tsx\nimport React, { useRef } from 'react';\nimport { QRCodeSVG, QRCodeCanvas } from 'qrcode.react';\n\ninterface ABHAQRProps {\n  abhaAddress: string;\n  patientName: string;\n}\n\nexport function PatientQRCode({ abhaAddress, patientName }: ABHAQRProps) {\n  const canvasRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const qrData = JSON.stringify({ abha: abhaAddress, name: patientName });\n\n  // Download QR code as PNG image\n  const handleDownload = () => {\n    const canvas = canvasRef.current?.querySelector('canvas');\n    if (!canvas) return;\n\n    const url = canvas.toDataURL('image/png');\n    const anchor = document.createElement('a');\n    anchor.href = url;\n    anchor.download = `ABHA-${abhaAddress}.png`;\n    anchor.click();\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"flex flex-col items-center gap-4 p-4 border rounded-xl bg-white shadow-sm\">\n      {/* 1. Scalable vector display for crisp UI presentation */}\n      <QRCodeSVG\n        value={qrData}\n        size={220}\n        level=\"H\" // High error correction level (recovers up to 30% damage/obscurity)\n        marginSize={2} // Clean margin border (quiet zone)\n        bgColor=\"#FFFFFF\"\n        fgColor=\"#0F172A\"\n        imageSettings={{\n          src: '/abdm-logo.png', // Center logo / watermark\n          height: 36,\n          width: 36,\n          excavate: true, // Clears background QR modules behind logo\n        }}\n      />\n\n      {/* 2. Hidden Canvas element for PNG export */}\n      <div ref={canvasRef} className=\"hidden\">\n        <QRCodeCanvas value={qrData} size={512} level=\"H\" marginSize={4} />\n      </div>\n\n      <button\n        onClick={handleDownload}\n        className=\"px-4 py-2 text-sm bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-lg hover:bg-slate-800 transition\"\n      >\n        Download QR Code\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Key Gotchas\n* **Named Imports in v4**: In older `qrcode.react` versions, default import was standard (`import QRCode from 'qrcode.react'`). In v4, use explicit named imports: `import { QRCodeSVG, QRCodeCanvas } from 'qrcode.react'`.\n* **Deprecated `includeMargin`**: The boolean `includeMargin` prop is deprecated in v4; use `marginSize={2}` or `marginSize={4}` (number of module widths).\n* **Logo Embedding Requires `level=\"H\"`**: When embedding logos or icons via `imageSettings`, the logo blocks data modules. Always set `level=\"H\"` (30% error recovery) or `level=\"Q\"` (25%). Setting `level=\"L\"` (7%) will render the QR code unscannable.\n* **Payload Density**: QR code dot density increases with data length. Avoid embedding raw base64 data or massive JSON objects directly into the QR value; use concise identifiers or URLs.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Supabase Edge Functions (Server-Side Operations & ABDM Integration)\n\n### Architecture Overview\n* **Runtime**: Deno edge runtime (Typescript native, globally distributed, sub-50ms cold starts).\n* **Security Model**: Secrets are securely stored in the Supabase Cloud vault and injected into `Deno.env` at execution time. Secrets are never exposed to browser clients.\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation for ABDM API\n\n#### 1. Set Secrets via Supabase CLI\n```bash\nsupabase secrets set ABDM_CLIENT_ID=\"sbx_client_12345\"\nsupabase secrets set ABDM_CLIENT_SECRET=\"sec_9876543210\"\nsupabase secrets set ABDM_BASE_URL=\"https://dev.abdm.gov.in\"\n```\n\nFor local testing, place these inside `supabase/functions/.env` (ensure `.env` is listed in `.gitignore`):\n```bash\nsupabase functions serve --env-file ./supabase/functions/.env\n```\n\n#### 2. Edge Function Code (`supabase/functions/abdm-gateway/index.ts`)\n```typescript\nimport { corsHeaders } from 'jsr:@supabase/supabase-js/cors';\nimport { createClient } from 'jsr:@supabase/supabase-js@2';\n\nDeno.serve(async (req: Request) => {\n  // 1. Mandatory CORS preflight check\n  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') {\n    return new Response('ok', { headers: corsHeaders });\n  }\n\n  try {\n    // 2. Validate user authentication token forwarded from client\n    const authHeader = req.headers.get('Authorization');\n    if (!authHeader) {\n      return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: 'Missing Authorization header' }), {\n        status: 401,\n        headers: { ...corsHeaders, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n      });\n    }\n\n    const supabase = createClient(\n      Deno.env.get('SUPABASE_URL')!,\n      Deno.env.get('SUPABASE_ANON_KEY')!,\n      { global: { headers: { Authorization: authHeader } } }\n    );\n\n    const { data: { user }, error: authError } = await supabase.auth.getUser();\n    if (authError || !user) {\n      return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: 'Unauthorized user token' }), {\n        status: 401,\n        headers: { ...corsHeaders, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n      });\n    }\n\n    // 3. Read protected ABDM secrets from Deno environment\n    const clientId = Deno.env.get('ABDM_CLIENT_ID');\n    const clientSecret = Deno.env.get('ABDM_CLIENT_SECRET');\n    const abdmBaseUrl = Deno.env.get('ABDM_BASE_URL') || 'https://dev.abdm.gov.in';\n\n    if (!clientId || !clientSecret) {\n      throw new Error('Server misconfiguration: ABDM credentials missing.');\n    }\n\n    // 4. Execute Server-to-Server call to ABDM Gateway\n    const abdmResponse = await fetch(`${abdmBaseUrl}/api/hiecm/gateway/v0.5/sessions`, {\n      method: 'POST',\n      headers: {\n        'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n        'REQUEST-ID': crypto.randomUUID(),\n        'TIMESTAMP': new Date().toISOString(),\n      },\n      body: JSON.stringify({ clientId, clientSecret }),\n      signal: AbortSignal.timeout(10000), // 10-second timeout guard\n    });\n\n    if (!abdmResponse.ok) {\n      const errorText = await abdmResponse.text();\n      return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: `ABDM gateway error: ${errorText}` }), {\n        status: abdmResponse.status,\n        headers: { ...corsHeaders, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n      });\n    }\n\n    const sessionData = await abdmResponse.json();\n\n    // 5. Return sanitized response to client\n    return new Response(\n      JSON.stringify({\n        success: true,\n        accessToken: sessionData.accessToken,\n        expiresIn: sessionData.expiresIn,\n      }),\n      {\n        status: 200,\n        headers: { ...corsHeaders, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n      }\n    );\n  } catch (err: any) {\n    return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: err.message || 'Internal error' }), {\n      status: 500,\n      headers: { ...corsHeaders, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n    });\n  }\n});\n```\n\n#### 3. Client Invocation in React\n```typescript\nimport { supabase } from './supabaseClient';\n\nexport async function getAbdmSessionToken() {\n  // Automatically attaches the current user's session JWT in the Authorization header\n  const { data, error } = await supabase.functions.invoke('abdm-gateway', {\n    body: { action: 'create_session' },\n  });\n\n  if (error) {\n    console.error('Failed to invoke ABDM Edge Function:', error);\n    throw error;\n  }\n\n  return data.accessToken;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Critical Gotchas for Edge Functions & ABDM\n* **CORS Preflight Failures**: Browsers automatically send an `OPTIONS` preflight request before `POST`. If your Edge Function does not intercept `req.method === 'OPTIONS'` and return `corsHeaders`, the browser rejects the response with a generic `CORS policy` error.\n* **Automatic Auth Header**: `supabase.functions.invoke()` automatically injects the logged-in user's JWT into `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Do not manually overwrite it unless calling unauthenticated endpoints.\n* **ABDM Gateway Downtime & Timeouts**: Government APIs (ABDM Sandbox) occasionally experience high latency. Edge Functions will terminate if hanging. Always wrap outgoing `fetch` calls in `AbortSignal.timeout(10000)` or `15000` to prevent unhandled worker crashes.\n* **ABDM Header Formatting**: ABDM APIs strictly enforce RFC 3339 UTC timestamps (e.g. `new Date().toISOString()`) and valid UUIDv4 strings (`crypto.randomUUID()`) in `REQUEST-ID`. Formatting discrepancies result in `400 Bad Request` errors.\n* **Deployment CLI**:\n  ```bash\n  supabase functions deploy abdm-gateway --no-verify-jwt # Use flag if endpoint is public, omit if Supabase gateway JWT verification is desired\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### Quick Reference Summary Table\n\n| Category | Primary Package | Current Latest Version | Key Recommendation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Frontend Setup** | `tailwindcss` + `@tailwindcss/vite` | `4.3.3` | Use `@import \"tailwindcss\";` and `@theme` in CSS. No `tailwind.config.js`. |\n| **Supabase SDK** | `@supabase/supabase-js` | `2.117.0` | Use `signInWithOtp` / `verifyOtp`. Wrap `(SELECT auth.uid())` in RLS policies. |\n| **Client OCR** | `tesseract.js` | `6.0.1` | Pass `['hin', 'eng']` to `createWorker`. Use Singleton Worker to prevent WASM leaks. |\n| **Localization** | `react-i18next` + `i18next-http-backend` | `17.0.15` / `4.0.2` | Place translation JSON in `public/locales/`. Wrap app in React 19 `<Suspense>`. |\n| **Voice Input** | Web Speech API (`SpeechRecognition`) | Native Browser API | Works well on Chrome desktop/Android with `hi-IN`; fails on Firefox; use fallback for clinical accuracy. |\n| **QR Code** | `qrcode.react` | `4.2.0` | Use named `QRCodeSVG` / `QRCodeCanvas`. Use `level=\"H\"` when embedding logos. |\n| **Backend Ops** | Supabase Edge Functions | Deno Runtime | Store ABDM keys in `supabase secrets`. Implement explicit CORS `OPTIONS` handler. |\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
